\title{Naïve #{n}-bit multiplication}
\date{2026-10-22T12:25:25Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{What about multiplication?  Using the standard "grade school"
algorithm to compute #{p \times q}, we end up with a shifted copy of
#{p} for every #{1} bit in #{q}, and then we must add then all.  So
there are up to #{n} shifted copies of #{p} to add, with each being at
most #{2n} bits, so this particular algorithm will take #{\Theta(n^2)}
in the worst case.}

\p{However, unlike [addition](00PT) which obviously takes #{\Omega(n)} time, it is not so obvious that multiplication must take #{\Omega(n^2)} time.  In fact, if anything, it is the opposite: it seems like adding up a bunch of shifted copies of the same bitstring might involve some redundant work.  Perhaps there is a way we can multiply two #{n}-bit numbers in faster than quadratic time.}
